#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078741 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c74cb8d2-be8f-5489-972e-da83ac768671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{3}{4}$ gallons per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075748} {:answer/id #uuid "00681b24-9269-5b08-98d7-d964e2bc10d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{2}$ gallons per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075749} {:answer/id #uuid "00ff1e36-2d4e-5116-86d0-bf242f2b5cb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ gallons per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075750} {:answer/id #uuid "0e8a3c6f-7fa6-56db-b043-5d9c6ee643ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{3}{4}$ gallons per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075751} {:answer/id #uuid "4260e202-6dd1-5ae0-8acd-16a40e53003c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{4}$ gallons per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4260e202-6dd1-5ae0-8acd-16a40e53003c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{4}$ gallons per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075752} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d179e5a-e720-56ea-8484-baad29151810" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075747}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b028592-a2cc-5218-a00a-86601c159f40" :question/math-academy-id "q-2436" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe volume $V$, in gallons, of water in a pool $t$ minutes after the pool starts filling can be modeled by the function $V(t) = 5\\sqrt{t} + 2$. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the water volume when $t = 4$ minutes?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the water volume when $t=4$ minutes is equal to ${V}^{′}(4).$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\sqrt{t}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{t}^{\\frac{1}{2}}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{-\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the water volume when $t=4$ minutes is $\\frac{5}{4}$ gallons per minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93aaae5b-304c-5cc1-a128-c608c839095a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266157} {:answer/id #uuid "04a592ec-a5b2-5245-911f-4ade8d3308bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04a592ec-a5b2-5245-911f-4ade8d3308bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266158} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ea2b541-37cc-5099-9982-5472f6a7753c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266156}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c1266d9-11b2-531e-8076-104154430ad8" :question/math-academy-id "q-341053" :question/problem "The height $h$ (in feet) of a small rocket $t$ seconds after it is launched can be modeled by the function $h(t)=-16{t}^{2}+128t.$ Find the instantaneous rate at which the rocket's height is changing with respect to time at the moment when its height is equal to $256$ feet.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change of the rocket's height when its height is $256$ feet is\n{{field-1}} feet per second." :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the rocket's height is $256$ feet, the time can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(t) & =256 \\\\ -16{t}^{2}+128t & =256 \\\\ -16{t}^{2}+128t-256 & =0 \\\\ -16({t}^{2}-8t+16) & =0 \\\\ -16(t-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (t-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ t-4 & =0 \\\\ t & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe instantaneous rate at which the rocket's height is changing when $t=4$ seconds is equal to ${h}^{′}(4).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $h(t)$ with respect to $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-16{t}^{2}+128t) \\\\  & =-32t+128,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $t=4$ gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(4)=-32(4)+128=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the rocket's height is changing when $t=4$ seconds is $0$ feet per second."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55742b20-d2eb-52dd-93ba-2bea32a81289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55742b20-d2eb-52dd-93ba-2bea32a81289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266161} :answer-field/id #uuid "181d8716-3c0a-5aff-9c35-c6ef0ef17dc0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266160}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bcb5129b-3804-548c-a47a-d6b56b122986" :question/math-academy-id "q-343424" :question/problem "The concentration $C$ (in millimoles per liter) of a chemical in a reactor $t$ minutes after the reaction begins is modeled by the function $C(t)={t}^{3}+25$ for $0\\le t\\le 10.$ Find the instantaneous rate at which the concentration is changing with respect to time at the moment when the concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change when the concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter is\n{{field-1}} millimoles per liter per minute." :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the chemical's concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter, the time can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(t) & =150 \\\\ {t}^{3}+25 & =150 \\\\ {t}^{3} & =125 \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe instantaneous rate at which the chemical's concentration is changing when $t=5$ minutes is equal to ${C}^{′}(5).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $C(t)$ with respect to $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+25) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $t=5$ gives\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(5)=3(5)^{2}=75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the chemical's concentration when its concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter is  $75$ millimoles per liter per minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38ba7e5a-239f-50a6-b2cd-33566d3c95f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266164} {:answer/id #uuid "9e240240-0e38-5d6c-9044-c0aa67b65783" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38ba7e5a-239f-50a6-b2cd-33566d3c95f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266164} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fd21b00-71b4-51a2-a3d9-e416a5f840d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266163}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d10a00cb-697c-5a2f-9559-19cd6f8f6ef5" :question/math-academy-id "q-343496" :question/problem "The lateral surface area $A$ of a cone is increasing. The radius of the base of the cone is $r,$ and the slant height of the cone is always twice its radius. Find the rate of change of the lateral surface area of the cone with respect to its radius.\n\nThe rate of change of the lateral surface area of the cone with respect to its radius $r$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The area of the lateral surface area of the cone is given by\n\n$$\nA=A(r)=\\pi rℓ,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the base of the cone and $ℓ$ is its slant height.\n\nWe are given that $ℓ=2r,$ so we can express the area $A$ as a function of the radius $r$ by substituting $ℓ=2r$ into the formula above. We get\n\n$$\nA=\\pi r(2r)=2\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $A(r)$ with respect to its radius $r$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(2\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (2r) \\\\  & =4\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbe2982f-a752-54b7-b0f4-c50b37c260b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbe2982f-a752-54b7-b0f4-c50b37c260b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266168} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e113cfa-03c5-531e-9d48-c21ef10c1193" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266167}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce974f27-56cb-5d6d-a571-c1f4112d1075" :question/math-academy-id "q-343502" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953428/assets/5fc1aff711355a147abac33a513434c55575c552ab58f0164ebc87222530f371.png)\n\nThe volume of a cube-shaped container is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the container is changing with respect to the side length at the moment when the face diagonal of the cube is equal to $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at the moment when the face diagonal is $10\\sqrt{2}$ centimeters is equal to\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the face diagonal of the cube is $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}$, its side length $s$ is $10\\,\\text{cm}.$ Now, the volume of a cube with side length $s$ is given by \n\n$$\nV=V(s)={s}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $V(s)$ with respect to $s$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}({s}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{s}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $s=10$ gives\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(10)=3(10)^{2}=300.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the container is increasing when the face diagonal is $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}$ is $300\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}.$"}]}
